// 116 — Inline / intrinsic functions — DESIGN NOTE
// `inline` in C/C++ is a *hint* to the compiler; modern compilers
// largely ignore it and decide via cost model. "Intrinsic" usually
// means a function the compiler recognizes by name and lowers to a
// specific instruction sequence (`__builtin_popcount`,
// `_mm_add_epi32`, etc.).
//
// AiLang has intrinsics in the second sense — Add, Subtract,
// Multiply, Divide, Modulo, And, Or, Xor, Shl, Shr, EqualTo, etc.
// are recognized by the codegen and emitted as direct x86-64
// instructions, not function calls. There's no user-side `inline`
// keyword because there's no user-side mechanism to opt into it;
// every Function compiles to a real call (the codegen doesn't
// inline user functions today).
//
// If/when an inliner lands, it will most likely be cost-model-driven
// — no `Inline.Foo` keyword. The current path to "make this faster"
// is: keep the hot loop's body short, avoid unnecessary calls inside
// it, and let the existing intrinsics carry the arithmetic.
SubRoutine.Main {
    // The Add below is an intrinsic — emits ADD instruction directly,
    // no call/ret overhead. Add, Multiply, etc. are the entire list.
    a = 100
    b = 200
    PrintMessage("a + b = ")
    PrintNumber(Add(a, b))
    PrintMessage("\n")

    // A user Function call, by contrast, is a real CALL/RET pair.
    PrintMessage("(intrinsics are inlined; user Functions are real calls)\n")
}
RunTask(Main)
